<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaffna Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1215 CE – 1619 CE (approx.) · capital Nallur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tamil kingdom of northern Sri Lanka ruled by the Aryacakravarti dynasty from Nallur until the Portuguese conquest in 1619.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> peninsula emerged after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga-magha">Kalinga Magha's</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a> around 1215. By the late 13th century it was held by the Aryacakravarti dynasty, which originated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> commanders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> area and established itself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur">Nallur</a> under the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At its height in the 14th century the kingdom controlled the pearl fisheries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a> and collected tribute from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vanni-chieftaincies">Vanni chieftaincies</a> and parts of the Sinhalese west coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited its king in 1344. The court supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur-kandaswamy-kovil">Nallur Kandaswamy</a>, and Tamil literature on astrology and medicine.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kotte">Kingdom of Kotte</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> from about 1450 to 1467. In the 16th century the Portuguese intervened repeatedly, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankili-ii">Cankili II's</a> resistance they annexed the kingdom in 1619, adding it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-ceylon">Portuguese Ceylon</a>. Jaffna remains the cultural heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamil</a> society.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaffna-kingdom.jpg" alt="Jaffna Kingdom" width="320" height="396"><figcaption>Image: AntanO via Taprobanus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaffna_Kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1215 CE – 1619 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nallur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cankili I, Cankili II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aryacakravarti dynasty, Kingdom of Jaffna, Kingdom of Yalpanam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1417&amp;focus=jaffna-kingdom" aria-label="Jaffna Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6888 1817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaffna Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7208 2073L7240 2137M7208 2073L7230 2166M7208 2073L7154 2063M7208 2073L7212 2176M7208 2073L7211 2175"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7240 2137h0M7230 2166h0M7154 2063h0M7212 2176h0M7211 2175h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7208 2073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7208 2073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nallur. <a href="/atlas?year=1417&amp;focus=jaffna-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

